\\nIf you expect the page to be framed only by pages on your server (e.g. it's part of a FRAMESET) then you'll want to use SAMEORIGIN, otherwise if you never expect the page to be framed, you should use DENY. Alternatively consider implementing Content Security Policy's \\"frame-ancestors\\" directive.", "sourceid": "3", "messageId": "1", "reference": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/X-Frame-Options", "confidence": "Medium", "description": "The response does not protect against 'ClickJacking' attacks. It should include either Content-Security-Policy with 'frame-ancestors' directive or X-Frame-Options.", "inputVector": "", "sourceMessageId": 1}	\N	pending	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	2026-07-03 10:17:11.795522+00	http://zero.webappsecurity.com/	\N	1021	\N	x-frame-options	0	\N	Modern Web browsers support the Content-Security-Policy and X-Frame-Options HTTP headers. Ensure one of them is set on all web pages returned by your site/app.\nIf you expect the page to be framed only by pages on your server (e.g. it's part of a FRAMESET) then you'll want to use SAMEORIGIN, otherwise if you never expect the page to be framed, you should use DENY. Alternatively consider implementing Content Security Policy's "frame-ancestors" directive.	https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/X-Frame-Options	\N	15	\N	\N	{"params": ["x-frame-options"]}
